WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.625 --> 00:00:03.420
(host)
Imagine being the mother
to every single living thing.

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:05.296
What would you do
if one of your children

00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:07.090
threatened the safety
of the others?

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:11.094
Would you kill or imprison
your progeny to save the world?

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:12.554
It's an impossible choice,

00:00:12.637 --> 00:00:14.389
one that no mother
wants to make,

00:00:14.472 --> 00:00:17.767
but maybe it gets easier
after the first time.

00:00:17.851 --> 00:00:18.977
Gaia would know.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.312
As Greek mythology's
original Mother Earth,

00:00:21.396 --> 00:00:24.649
with the unique ability to
create life from nothing,

00:00:24.733 --> 00:00:27.402
Gaia made the choice to
fight against her descendants

00:00:27.485 --> 00:00:29.195
three separate times.

00:00:29.279 --> 00:00:31.406
And in typical
Greek mythology fashion,

00:00:31.489 --> 00:00:35.160
this feminine figure doesn't
get a very satisfying ending.

00:00:35.243 --> 00:00:39.372
[curious instrumental music]

00:00:39.456 --> 00:00:42.917
In art, Gaia was often
depicted as one with the land,

00:00:43.001 --> 00:00:45.712
either a woman's torso
emerging from the soil,

00:00:45.795 --> 00:00:48.256
or as a woman lounging
on the very ground

00:00:48.339 --> 00:00:49.424
that she personified.

00:00:49.507 --> 00:00:52.594
In stories, she was described
as a beautiful woman

00:00:52.677 --> 00:00:54.721
with a buxom, motherly figure.

00:00:54.804 --> 00:00:58.057
Hesiod called her
"wide-bosomed" in his Theogony.

00:00:58.141 --> 00:01:00.351
But all of these terms
are just different ways

00:01:00.435 --> 00:01:02.854
to say that Mother Earth
had a lot of curves.

00:01:02.937 --> 00:01:04.522
Unlike the Olympian gods,

00:01:04.606 --> 00:01:07.317
who had power
over specific domains,

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.694
Gaia's abilities
were more general.

00:01:09.778 --> 00:01:11.529
As the personification
of Earth,

00:01:11.613 --> 00:01:14.991
Gaia had some control
over the land and weather.

00:01:15.074 --> 00:01:16.993
She also had
the power of prophecy

00:01:17.076 --> 00:01:19.871
and was said to share her
predictions through dreams.

00:01:19.954 --> 00:01:23.416
These oneiric messages were
just one of many presents

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:25.376
that Gaia bestowed
on her followers,

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:29.172
and so she was appropriately
given the epithet "Anesidora,"

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:32.258
which meant "spender"
or "giver of gifts."

00:01:32.342 --> 00:01:34.427
Gaia's most important
ability, however,

00:01:34.511 --> 00:01:37.263
was the power to create life
even without the...

00:01:37.347 --> 00:01:38.765
usual methods.

00:01:38.848 --> 00:01:40.642
It is because
of this very power

00:01:40.725 --> 00:01:43.436
that the Greek pantheon
existed at all.

00:01:43.520 --> 00:01:45.355
According to
Greek creation myths,

00:01:45.438 --> 00:01:48.358
the universe at first
held nothing but Chaos,

00:01:48.441 --> 00:01:50.360
a lifeless bulk of darkness

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:53.112
from which Gaia
spontaneously formed.

00:01:53.196 --> 00:01:56.115
Tartarus and Eros
also sprang from Chaos,

00:01:56.199 --> 00:01:57.534
and together with Gaia,

00:01:57.617 --> 00:01:59.869
these three were
the Protogenoi,

00:01:59.953 --> 00:02:03.081
Greek mythology's
most primordial deities.

00:02:03.164 --> 00:02:05.750
With the pit of Tartarus
resting below her,

00:02:05.834 --> 00:02:08.545
Gaia birthed Ourea to
look over the mountains

00:02:08.628 --> 00:02:10.588
and Pontus
to care for the seas,

00:02:10.672 --> 00:02:13.800
and earthly creatures
sprang from her skin.

00:02:13.883 --> 00:02:15.385
In an act
that would bring about

00:02:15.468 --> 00:02:18.304
a thousand mythological
years worth of drama,

00:02:18.388 --> 00:02:20.849
Gaia also gave birth
to Ouranos,

00:02:20.932 --> 00:02:22.642
a personification
of the sky

00:02:22.725 --> 00:02:24.894
who stretched
above her in a dome.

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:27.897
Gaia and Ouranos produced
many powerful offspring

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:29.899
in the more traditional way.

00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:32.151
Together they bore
12 Titan children,

00:02:32.235 --> 00:02:36.197
including Rhea and Cronus,
then three giant Cyclopeses,

00:02:36.281 --> 00:02:37.949
and three Hecatoncheires,

00:02:38.032 --> 00:02:40.702
who were just as large as
their one-eyed brothers,

00:02:40.785 --> 00:02:43.913
but each had 50 heads
and 100 arms.

00:02:43.997 --> 00:02:46.791
Ouranos felt threatened
by his out-sized children

00:02:46.875 --> 00:02:50.295
and hid them away
so deep in Gaia's own womb

00:02:50.378 --> 00:02:52.547
that they ended up
in the pit of Tartarus,

00:02:52.630 --> 00:02:54.507
the darkest part of Hades.

00:02:54.591 --> 00:02:57.802
Naturally, Gaia was
upset with the situation.

00:02:57.886 --> 00:02:59.721
Maybe Ouranos
could have gotten away

00:02:59.804 --> 00:03:01.431
with punishing the Giants

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:03.975
or with forcing Gaia to
suffer painful cramps,

00:03:04.058 --> 00:03:05.810
but certainly not both.

00:03:05.894 --> 00:03:07.854
Gaia conspired
with her Titan children

00:03:07.937 --> 00:03:09.606
to overthrow their father,

00:03:09.689 --> 00:03:11.941
and the death blow
was dealt by Cronus,

00:03:12.025 --> 00:03:13.902
who castrated Ouranos
with a sickle

00:03:13.985 --> 00:03:15.695
made by Gaia's own hand

00:03:15.778 --> 00:03:18.323
and threw his genitals
into the sea.

00:03:18.406 --> 00:03:21.659
The blood from this
violent act produced Giants,

00:03:21.743 --> 00:03:24.662
the goddess Aphrodite,
and the Furies.

00:03:24.746 --> 00:03:26.414
With Ouranos
out of the picture,

00:03:26.497 --> 00:03:28.875
Cronus took over as king
of the heavens.

00:03:28.958 --> 00:03:32.003
Isn't it funny how history
tends to repeat itself?

00:03:32.086 --> 00:03:33.504
Gaia probably
wouldn't think so,

00:03:33.588 --> 00:03:37.383
but even a powerful earth
goddess can't outrun fate.

00:03:37.467 --> 00:03:39.302
Gaia foresaw
that her son Cronus

00:03:39.385 --> 00:03:42.055
would one day be
overthrown by his own son.

00:03:42.138 --> 00:03:44.891
When he heard the prophecy,
Cronus tried to avoid it

00:03:44.974 --> 00:03:46.976
by eating
all his newborn children

00:03:47.060 --> 00:03:49.395
whom he sired
with his sister Rhea.

00:03:49.479 --> 00:03:51.773
At least he learned from
his father's mistake

00:03:51.856 --> 00:03:55.068
and didn't try to imprison
them within Gaia's belly.

00:03:55.151 --> 00:03:58.905
But she was no more thrilled
by her son's casual cannibalism

00:03:58.988 --> 00:04:02.158
than she was by Ouranos
playing the unfair jailer,

00:04:02.241 --> 00:04:04.577
so Gaia conspired
with her daughter Rhea

00:04:04.661 --> 00:04:06.704
to dethrone the cruel Cronus.

00:04:06.788 --> 00:04:08.289
The Earth Mother
advised Rhea

00:04:08.373 --> 00:04:10.708
to hide
her sixth-born child, Zeus,

00:04:10.792 --> 00:04:13.086
and to give Cronus
a blanket-wrapped stone

00:04:13.169 --> 00:04:14.671
to swallow instead.

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:16.255
Zeus was raised in secret

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:19.258
in a cave on Mount Dikti
on the island Crete.

00:04:19.342 --> 00:04:22.095
By some accounts,
Gaia raised Zeus herself,

00:04:22.178 --> 00:04:25.139
though others say
he was raised by nymphs.

00:04:25.223 --> 00:04:27.767
When he was old enough,
Zeus returned to Olympus

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:31.229
and used a special
regurgitory elixir from Gaia

00:04:31.312 --> 00:04:34.148
to free his brothers and
sisters from Cronus's gut.

00:04:34.232 --> 00:04:38.111
With Gaia's help, Zeus and the
other would-be Olympian gods

00:04:38.194 --> 00:04:40.738
overthrew the Titans and
imprisoned many of them

00:04:40.822 --> 00:04:41.948
in Tartarus.

00:04:42.031 --> 00:04:44.909
Angered again by
her children's confinement,

00:04:44.993 --> 00:04:48.287
Gaia urged her Giant children
to attack the Olympians,

00:04:48.371 --> 00:04:51.624
but they were defeated after
the gods enlisted Herakles,

00:04:51.708 --> 00:04:53.668
for it was prophesized
that the Giants

00:04:53.751 --> 00:04:56.462
could only be thwarted
with a mortal's help.

00:04:56.546 --> 00:04:59.465
When that attempt failed,
Gaia lay with Tartarus

00:04:59.549 --> 00:05:02.885
to produce a truly
terrifying offspring:

00:05:02.969 --> 00:05:05.555
Typhus, the father
of all monsters,

00:05:05.638 --> 00:05:08.766
who had 100
fire-breathing dragon heads.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:11.102
Typhus almost defeated
the gods

00:05:11.185 --> 00:05:14.063
but was eventually struck down
by Zeus's lightning bolt.

00:05:14.147 --> 00:05:16.899
Not wanting to endanger
any more of her children,

00:05:16.983 --> 00:05:20.486
Gaia accepted Zeus's role
as the ruler of Olympus.

00:05:20.570 --> 00:05:22.822
When we talk about
ancient mythology now,

00:05:22.905 --> 00:05:25.825
it's tempting to treat it
as a static story.

00:05:25.908 --> 00:05:29.787
We see the end state and assume
that it was always that way.

00:05:29.871 --> 00:05:32.957
But myths, like history,
evolve over time,

00:05:33.041 --> 00:05:36.085
and Gaia's story is
an excellent example of that.

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:37.712
Most people think of Zeus

00:05:37.795 --> 00:05:40.131
as the highest God
in Greek mythology,

00:05:40.214 --> 00:05:43.885
but it was once Cronus
and, before him, Ouranos.

00:05:43.968 --> 00:05:47.513
But they all got their power
and position thanks to Gaia,

00:05:47.597 --> 00:05:49.140
who never really got to rule

00:05:49.223 --> 00:05:51.809
over the Earth that
she was responsible for.

00:05:51.893 --> 00:05:54.645
And even though Gaia
played a pivotal role

00:05:54.729 --> 00:05:56.522
at the beginning
of the Greek myths,

00:05:56.606 --> 00:06:00.818
she doesn't get any kind of
satisfying ending, good or bad.

00:06:00.902 --> 00:06:02.779
She seems to disappear
from the myths

00:06:02.862 --> 00:06:04.655
after Zeus's wedding to Hera,

00:06:04.739 --> 00:06:08.242
without even the honor of
a memorable, horrific punishment

00:06:08.326 --> 00:06:10.620
like so many
of her Titan offspring.

00:06:10.703 --> 00:06:13.956
A modern Western lens would
blame that on the patriarchy,

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:15.416
saying a culture that worships

00:06:15.500 --> 00:06:17.752
such a male-dominated pantheon

00:06:17.835 --> 00:06:19.420
would downplay
the accomplishments

00:06:19.504 --> 00:06:21.631
of the ultimate
maternal figure.

00:06:21.714 --> 00:06:25.051
There's some truth in that,
but it's not the whole story.

00:06:25.134 --> 00:06:27.178
Gaia's exclusion
from most myths

00:06:27.261 --> 00:06:28.971
has led some researchers
to believe

00:06:29.055 --> 00:06:31.015
that she is
a much older figure

00:06:31.099 --> 00:06:33.309
than any of the Olympian gods.

00:06:33.392 --> 00:06:35.520
Figurines
of motherly goddesses

00:06:35.603 --> 00:06:39.398
have been found at many ancient
shrines associated with Gaia,

00:06:39.482 --> 00:06:41.901
and they were
very reminiscent of statues

00:06:41.984 --> 00:06:44.237
from neolithic
and Bronze Age times

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:46.572
representing the Great Mother.

00:06:46.656 --> 00:06:48.241
Similar statues have been found

00:06:48.324 --> 00:06:51.828
in modern-day Turkey,
Germany, and France.

00:06:51.911 --> 00:06:53.913
Folklorist Jules Cashford
claims

00:06:53.996 --> 00:06:55.665
that the myth of Gaia
has roots

00:06:55.748 --> 00:06:58.459
in the ancient Indian
figure Gayatri,

00:06:58.543 --> 00:07:00.711
who was brought to Europe
by the Mycenaeans

00:07:00.795 --> 00:07:02.922
about 4,000 years ago.

00:07:03.005 --> 00:07:06.134
Gaia's underwhelming presence
in mythology

00:07:06.217 --> 00:07:08.302
could be a consequence
of conquest

00:07:08.386 --> 00:07:10.221
or a matter of misogyny,

00:07:10.304 --> 00:07:12.723
or maybe she just needed
a break from her kids

00:07:12.807 --> 00:07:15.977
like any overworked mom
and lost track of time

00:07:16.060 --> 00:07:17.770
as immortals are wont to do.

00:07:17.854 --> 00:07:18.813
Whatever the reason

00:07:18.896 --> 00:07:21.274
for her relative absence
from later myths,

00:07:21.357 --> 00:07:24.527
Gaia was worshiped at
several sites across Greece,

00:07:24.610 --> 00:07:26.779
including Athens and Delphi.

00:07:26.863 --> 00:07:29.490
Erichthonius,
an early king of Athens,

00:07:29.574 --> 00:07:33.703
was said to be the ill-begotten
son of Gaia by Hephaestus,

00:07:33.786 --> 00:07:36.664
and so he decreed that Gaia
would receive sacrifices

00:07:36.747 --> 00:07:39.375
before any other deity.

00:07:39.458 --> 00:07:40.668
As a chthonic goddess

00:07:40.751 --> 00:07:43.212
associated with the earth
and the underworld,

00:07:43.296 --> 00:07:46.090
the animals sacrificed
to her had to be black.

00:07:46.174 --> 00:07:48.885
The Oracle at Delphi
ultimately came to be connected

00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:51.262
to the God Apollo,
but legend says

00:07:51.345 --> 00:07:53.514
it originally
belonged to Gaia.

00:07:53.598 --> 00:07:55.099
In the form of a tree,

00:07:55.183 --> 00:07:58.102
Gaia breathed life into
the Sybilline stone

00:07:58.186 --> 00:08:00.730
and gave it her power
of prophecy.

00:08:00.813 --> 00:08:03.941
Gaia eventually renounced
her claim on the oracle stone,

00:08:04.025 --> 00:08:06.277
and others took over its power.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:09.780
Gaia was also venerated at
many of Demeter's temples,

00:08:09.864 --> 00:08:12.700
and some scholars believe
that these two goddesses

00:08:12.783 --> 00:08:14.202
may have once been worshiped

00:08:14.285 --> 00:08:17.663
as a single agricultural
and fertility deity.

00:08:17.747 --> 00:08:20.249
Gaia's origins
and her mythological fate

00:08:20.333 --> 00:08:21.918
may be unclear to us,

00:08:22.001 --> 00:08:25.296
but her influence on
today's culture is undeniable.

00:08:25.379 --> 00:08:28.049
Modern Wiccans
and Pagans revere Gaia

00:08:28.132 --> 00:08:29.967
in her role as Mother Earth.

00:08:30.051 --> 00:08:34.430
An ecological framework called
the Gaia Hypothesis from 1979

00:08:34.514 --> 00:08:37.683
saw the environment as
a self-sustaining system,

00:08:37.767 --> 00:08:41.562
almost as if it were guided
by a living, nurturing hand.

00:08:41.646 --> 00:08:45.149
In 2013, scientists launched
a telescope into space

00:08:45.233 --> 00:08:46.984
and then ironically named it

00:08:47.068 --> 00:08:49.445
after this primordial
Earth goddess.

00:08:49.528 --> 00:08:51.405
In this latest incarnation,

00:08:51.489 --> 00:08:53.658
the Gaia space Telescope
gave astronomers

00:08:53.741 --> 00:08:56.827
the most accurate map
of stars in our galaxy

00:08:56.911 --> 00:08:58.412
that we've ever seen,

00:08:58.496 --> 00:09:00.456
measuring the positions
and velocities

00:09:00.539 --> 00:09:02.750
of more than
one billion stars,

00:09:02.833 --> 00:09:05.920
and gave me the data
that I used to get my PhD.

00:09:06.003 --> 00:09:08.714
Whether she's serving as
the foundation and engine

00:09:08.798 --> 00:09:11.092
of our planet's
entire ecosystem

00:09:11.175 --> 00:09:13.886
or unveiling the mysteries
of the universe,

00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:16.973
Gaia has always
looked out for us humans.

00:09:17.056 --> 00:09:18.933
She made the impossible choice

00:09:19.016 --> 00:09:21.060
to dismember
her immortal children

00:09:21.143 --> 00:09:24.897
to save the world from
their cruelty time after time,

00:09:24.981 --> 00:09:26.941
until she couldn't
fight anymore.

00:09:27.024 --> 00:09:29.527
And yet, she's seen
as the prime antagonist

00:09:29.610 --> 00:09:33.155
of Greek mythology; that is,
if she's remembered at all.

00:09:33.239 --> 00:09:36.659
So remember Gaia, and respect
the Earth that she represents,

00:09:36.742 --> 00:09:38.536
and for all our sakes,

00:09:38.619 --> 00:09:40.871
stop putting
her children in jail!

00:09:40.955 --> 00:09:44.000
Accessibility provided by the
U.S. Department of Education.

